{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Understanding the Concepts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 1. Device Tree Overlays\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b What they are:} - Text files describing hardware to Linux kernel - Tell kernel \u8220"this hardware exists at this location\u8221" - Required because CSI cameras aren\u8217't auto-detectable like USB\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b How they work:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Hardware \u8594 ? Device Tree Overlay \u8594 ? Kernel Driver \u8594 ? libcamera \u8594 ? Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Example:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 dtoverlay=imx708,cam0\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This tells kernel: - There\u8217's an IMX708 sensor - Connected to CAM0 port - Load appropriate driver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 2. libcamera Architecture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b What is libcamera:} - Modern camera framework for Linux - Replaces old V4L2 approach - Supports complex camera systems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Pipeline concept:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Sensor \u8594 ? CSI \u8594 ? ISP \u8594 ? Memory \u8594 ? Application\line
   \u8595 ?      \u8595 ?     \u8595 ?      \u8595 ?         \u8595 ?\line
  IMX708  RP1  PiSP  DMA    rpicam-apps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why Raspberry Pi fork:} - Upstream libcamera = generic, no Pi support - Raspberry Pi fork = adds Pi-specific pipelines - Pipeline handlers for RP1 chip (Pi 5) and ISP\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3. DMA and Memory Management\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b DMA (Direct Memory Access):}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Without DMA:          With DMA:\line
Camera \u8594 ? CPU \u8594 ? RAM    Camera \u8594 ? RAM (direct)\line
  (slow, CPU busy)      (fast, CPU free)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b CMA (Contiguous Memory Allocator):} - Video frames need continuous memory blocks - Can\u8217't be fragmented across multiple locations - Must reserve at boot time - Our setting: 512MB reserved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why allocation fails without CMA:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Normal memory: [A][B][C][D][E]  \u8592 ? Fragmented\line
CMA memory:    [AAAAA]           \u8592 ? Continuous block for camera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 4. OpenCV Bridge Architecture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why direct access fails:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 OpenCV \u8594 ? V4L2 (/dev/video0) \u10007 ? \u8594 ? libcamera camera \u8592 ? Wrong interface!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Our solution:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Camera \u8594 ? libcamera \u8594 ? rpicam-vid \u8594 ? MJPEG stream \u8594 ? Python buffer \u8594 ?\line
  \u8594 ? JPEG decode \u8594 ? OpenCV frame \u8594 ? cv2.imshow()\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b MJPEG encoding:} - Each frame = complete JPEG image - Easy to find boundaries (0xFFD8\u8230 ?0xFFD9) - Good compression - Decodable by OpenCV\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Alternative (YUV):} - Raw uncompressed frames - Faster (no JPEG encode/decode) - More CPU for large resolutions - More complex to handle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5. Dual Camera System\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b How Pi 5 handles two cameras:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 \u9484 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9488 ?\line
\u9474 ?   Raspberry Pi 5 \u9474 ?\line
\u9474 ?                  \u9474 ?\line
\u9474 ?  \u9484 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9488 ?         \u9474 ?\line
\u9474 ?  \u9474 ? RP1 \u9474 ?         \u9474 ?\line
\u9474 ?  \u9492 ?\u9472 ?\u9516 ?\u9472 ?\u9516 ?\u9472 ?\u9496 ?         \u9474 ?\line
\u9474 ?    \u9474 ? \u9474 ?           \u9474 ?\line
\u9474 ?  CAM0 CAM1       \u9474 ?\line
\u9474 ?    \u9474 ?   \u9474 ?         \u9474 ?\line
\u9492 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9532 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9532 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ?\u9496 ?\line
     \u9474 ?   \u9474 ?\line
   IMX708 ToF\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b RP1 chip:} - Manages both CSI lanes - Dual Image Signal Processors (ISPs) - Can process both cameras simultaneously - Limited only by memory bandwidth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Camera numbering:} - Camera 0 = First in config.txt (our IMX708) - Camera 1 = Second in config.txt (our ToF) - Can swap by changing order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 6. Object Detection Pipeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b YOLO processing flow:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 Camera frame (640x480 RGB)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
Resize to 416x416\line
    \u8595 ?\line
Normalize pixel values\line
    \u8595 ?\line
Feed to neural network\line
    \u8595 ?\line
Neural network processes (~200ms)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
Output: [class, confidence, bbox] for each detection\line
    \u8595 ?\line
Draw boxes on frame\line
    \u8595 ?\line
Display\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why it\u8217's slow:} - Neural network has ~7 million parameters - Each pixel processed multiple times - Raspberry Pi 5 CPU: ~2 GFLOPS - Modern GPU: ~10,000 GFLOPS - 5000x slower than GPU!\par}
